tmux — cheatsheet

The half of tmux that is sys's: three bindings that run sh/p, the two
options that make them work, the layout every pane lands in, and the
pointer that follows it. The prefix and the status line are your own
tmux.conf. Nothing here is unbound, so every tmux default still works
underneath.

Only what tmux can see is here. p's input is a word on the screen, which
no command can reach; the directory you are in is not, so opening it is
o at the prompt and needs no key at all.

the layer — unprefixed first, then the prefix and what takes it
  left-click    say the word under the pointer and stop there — it
                becomes cur, and nothing runs (p -c)
  right-click   open what the word under the pointer names (p)
  middle-click  send it to a prompt instead, as an argument (p -a)
  `             your prefix; press it twice to type one
  `p            in copy mode, the same for the cursor word; with a
                selection, for the selection
  `a            in copy mode, the same, sending instead of opening

Nothing here names fm, so changing sh/o changes what right-click and `p
open, as it changes o. fm's keys are in fm/cheatsheet.

p asks the file system, then git, and never lets screen text become a
command — the word travels in a pane option, not on a command line.

  file:line     ed at that line
  a path        o, which dispatches on the extension
  a url         browser
  a hash        git show
  none of it    search the pane backward for the text

p -c stops earlier than any of it: the word is resolved, said, and left
in cur, and the verb is whatever you type next — o, e, git add `{c}. A
left click is also what raises a pane, so every click says this; that is
the price of select being a click, and m is the refund.

p -a stops before that table and sends the path it resolved — absolute,
so it survives the trip — to the prompt of the marked pane (select-pane
-m), or the last one. No Enter, so the chord is: type wc -l, click the
word, press Return.

A path is one word only because word-separators is " ". With the
default set, the word under ed/2-motion/04-fwd.go:12 comes back as
`motion`, and the address is lost. That one line is the whole enabler.

focus-events is the second option, and it is fm's: a view that writes
cur has to know whether it is the pane being looked at, or a background
one takes the pronoun from the shell on its next redraw. tmux forwards
the terminal's focus in and out to the pane only with this on.

A path is relative to the directory that printed it, which is the
pane's own until someone cds — the reason p asks that pane, and the
reason fm opens there.

the layout — main-vertical, re-applied after every split
  The shell is master and everything else stacks beside it, at
  main-pane-width 50%. spawn asks for a pane and never says where one
  goes, so there is no side to get wrong and nothing to keep in sync.
  Your own splits land the same way, which is why ` % and ` " no
  longer pick a side; ` { and ` } promote a stack pane to master.
  The pointer follows the pane you land in, split or selected, so
  the next right-click is already there. It never moves when it is
  already in that pane, so a click is not dragged off the word.

